== Higher polynomial order basis functions

=== Quadratic basis functions

$u_e^h = sum_(A=1)^(N_(n_e)=3) N^A(x(xi)) d_e^A, w_e^h = sum_(A=1)^(N_(n_e)=3) N^A(x(xi)) c_e^A$

$N^1(xi) = - (xi (1 - xi))/2$

$N^2(xi) = 1-xi^2$

$N^3(xi) = (xi (1 + xi))/2$

Properties:

$N^A(xi^B) = delta_(A B)$

$sum_A N^A(xi) = 1$

=== Higher order basis functions generated by forula for Lagrange polynomials

$N_(n_e)-1$ - order polynomials

$N^A(xi) = (product_(B=1,B!=A)^N_(n_e) (xi - xi^B))/(product_(B=1,B!=A)^N_(n_e) (xi_A - xi_B)$

=== Develop the FE formaulation with quadratic basis functions

Dirichlet - Neumann Problem:

$sum_"e=1"^n_"el" integral_(Omega^e) w^h_",x" sigma^h A d x = sum_"e=1"^n_"el" integral_(Omega^e) w^h f A d x + w^h (L) t A$

Consider 

$ integral_(Omega^e) w^h_",x" E A u^h_(,x) d x = integral_(Omega^e) (sum_(A=1)^3 N^A_(,xi) xi_(,e) c^A_e) E A (sum_(A=1)^3 N^B_(,xi) xi_(,x) d^A_e) d x $

$u^h_",x"(xi) = sum_(A=1)^3 N^A_(,x) d^A_e, w^h_",x"(xi) = sum_(A=1)^3 N^A_(,x) c^A_e$

$N^1_(,xi) = dv(,xi) (1/2 -xi(1-xi)) = dv(,xi) 1/2 (xi-xi^2) = 1/2 (1-2 xi)$

$N^2_(,xi) = dv(,xi) (1-xi^2) = -2 xi$

$N^1_(,xi) = dv(,xi) (1/2 xi(1+xi)) = dv(,xi) 1/2 (xi+xi^2) = 1/2 (1+2 xi)$

To compute $xi_(,x)$ we ise tje omvertibility of mapping:

$x_e(xi) = sum_(A=1)^3 N^A(xi) x_e^A$

$ => x_(,xi) = sum_(A=1)^3 N^A_(,xi) x_e^A = N^1_(,xi) x^1_e + N^2_(,xi) x^2_e + N^3_(,xi) x^3_e$

$=> x_(,xi) = 1/2 ( -1 - 2 xi) x^1_e - 2 xi x^2_e + 1/2 (1 + 2 xi) x^3_e = (x^3_e - x^1_e)/2 + xi(x^1_e - 2 x^2_e + x^3_e)$

Because of the geometry of the x-Element:

$=> x_(,xi) = (x^3_e - x^1_e)/2 = h^e/2$

The fact that the map is invertible that

$xi_(,x) = 1/x_(,xi) = 2/h^e$, the tangent of the geometric map is constant. Affine map.

$=> integral_(Omega^e) w^h_",x" E A u^h_(,x) d x = integral_(Omega^e) (sum_(A=1)^3 N^A_(,xi) 2/h^e c^A_e) E A (sum_(B=1)^3 N^B_(,xi) 2/h^e d^B_e) d x $

$ = integral_(Omega^e) (sum_(A=1)^3 N^A_(,xi) 2/h^e c^A_e) E A (sum_(B=1)^3 N^B_(,xi) d^B_e) d xi$

$ = (2 E A)/h^e sum_((A,B) c^A_e (integral_(Omega^e) N^A_(,xi) N^B_(,xi) d xi) d^B_e$

$mat(c^1_e, c^2_e, c^3_e) (2 E A)/h^e mat(
  integral_(-1)^1 N^1_(,xi) N^1_(,xi) d xi, integral_(-1)^1 N^1_(,xi) N^2_(,xi) d xi, integral_(-1)^1 N^1_(,xi) N^3_(,xi) d xi;
  integral_(-1)^1 N^2_(,xi) N^1_(,xi) d xi, integral_(-1)^1 N^2_(,xi) N^2_(,xi) d xi, integral_(-1)^1 N^2_(,xi) N^3_(,xi) d xi;
  integral_(-1)^1 N^3_(,xi) N^1_(,xi) d xi, integral_(-1)^1 N^3_(,xi) N^2_(,xi) d xi, integral_(-1)^3 N^1_(,xi) N^3_(,xi) d xi;
  ) mat(d^1_e; d^2_e; d^3_e)$

Calculation of integrals:

$integral_(-1)^1 N^1_(,xi) N^1_(,xi) d xi = 1/4 integral_(-1)^1 (2 xi - 1)^2 d xi = 1/4 integral_(-1)^1 4 xi^2 - 4 xi + 1 d xi = 1/4 (4/3 xi^3 + xi) |_(-1)^1 = 7/6 $

$integral_(-1)^1 N^1_(,xi) N^2_(,xi) d xi = 1/2 integral_(-1)^1 (2 xi - 1) (-2 xi) d xi = 1/2 integral_(-1)^1 2 xi - 4 xi^2 d xi = 
  - 1/2 4/3 xi^3 |_(-1)^1 = - 4/3$

$integral_(-1)^1 N^1_(,xi) N^3_(,xi) d xi = 1/4 integral_(-1)^1 (2 xi - 1) (2 xi + 1) d xi = 1/4 integral_(-1)^1 (4 xi^2 - 1) d xi =
  1/4 (4/3 xi^3 - xi) |_(-1)^1 = 1/6$

$integral_(-1)^1 N^2_(,xi) N^2_(,xi) d xi = integral_(-1)^1 (-2 xi)^2 d xi = 4/3 xi^3 |_(-1)^1 = 8/3$

$integral_(-1)^1 N^2_(,xi) N^3_(,xi) d xi = 1/2 integral_(-1)^1 (-2 xi) (2 xi + 1) d xi =  
1/2 integral_(-1)^1 (-4 xi^2 - 2 xi) d xi = 1/2  (- 4/3 xi^3) |_(-1)^1 = -4/3$

$integral_(-1)^1 N^3_(,xi) N^3_(,xi) d xi = 1/4 integral_(-1)^1 (2 xi + 1)^2 d xi = 1/4 integral_(-1)^1 (4 xi^2 + 4 xi + 1) d xi = 
1/4 (4/3xi^3 + xi |_(-1)^1 = 7/6$

Collecting the result we get:

$integral_(Omega^e) w^h_",x" sigma^h A d x = 
mat(c^1_e, c^2_e, c^3_e) (2 E A)/h^e mat(
  7/6, -4/3, 1/6;
  -4/3, 8/3,-4/3;
  1/6, -4/3,7/6;
  ) mat(d^1_e; d^2_e; d^3_e)$

== The matrix-vector equations

$integral_(Omega^e) w^h f A d x = integral_(Omega^e) (sum_A N^A c^A_e) f A d x = sum_A c^A_e integral_(Omega^xi) N^A f A h^e/2 d xi$

Consider f, A are uniform over $Omega^e$

$ integral_(Omega^e) w^h f A d x = sum_(A=1)^3 c^A_e (f A h^e)/2 integral_(Omega^xi) N^A d xi$

Using matrix-vector notation:

$ integral_(Omega^e) w^h f A d x = 
  mat(c^1_e, c^2_e, c^3_e) (f A h^e)/2 mat( integral_(-1)^1 1/2 xi(xi-1) d xi;
                                            integral_(-1)^1 xi(1-xi)^2 d xi;
                                            integral_(-1)^1 1/2 xi(xi+1) d xi)$

$ integral_(Omega^e) w^h f A d x = 
  mat(c^1_e, c^2_e, c^3_e) (f A h^e)/2 mat( 1/3;
                                            4/3;
                                            1/3)$

Assembly of matrix vector equations

$sum_e integral_(Omega^e) w^h_",x" sigma^h A d x = sum_e integral_(Omega^e) w^h f A d x + w^h(L) t A$

Recall: For Dirichlet-Neumann problem $u^h(0) = u_0 = 0 => w^h(x) = 0$, this implies that for $e = 1, w^h_(e=1) = 
sum_(A=2)^3 N^A c^A_e = N^2 c^2 + N^3 c^3$

Global euation for the matrix vector weak form:

$mat(c^2, c^3) (2 E A)/h^1 mat(-4/3, 8/3, -4/3;
                               1/6, -1/3, 7/6) mat(d^1; d^2; d^3) + sum_(e=2)^(n_"el") (2 E A)/h^e
  mat(c^(2 e - 1), c^(2 e), c^(2 e + 1)) mat(7/6, -1/3, 1/6;
                    -4/3, 8/3, -4/3;
                    1/6, -1/3, 7/6) mat(d^1; d^2; d^3) = 
 mat(c^2, c^3) (f A h^1)/2 mat(4/3; 1/3) + sum_(e=2)^(n_"el") mat(c^(2 e - 1), c^(2 e), c^(2 e + 1)) (f A h^2)/2    
 mat(1/3;4/3;1/3) + e^(2 n_"el" + 1) t A$

== Numerical integration

Needed if $E, f, ....$ are complicated functions of position $x$ or if you use complicated basis functions.

=== Gausian Quadrature

Can be shown to be optimal for polynomials.

Consider $integral_(-1)^1 g(xi) d xi = sum:(l=1)^n_"int" g(xi_l) * w_l$, general form of quadrature rule.

$n_"int"$: number of integration points

$xi_l$: an integration point, $l = 1,...,n_"int"$

$w_l$: corresponding weight ascribed to the integration point, $sum_(l=1)^n_"int" w_l = 2$, this means that you can interate constants exactly.

$n_"int" = 1, xi_1 = 0, w_1 = 2$

$n_"int" = 2, xi_1 = -1/sqrt(3), w_1 = 1, xi_2 = 1/sqrt(3), w_2 = 1$

$n_"int" = 3, xi_1 = -sqrt(3/5), w_1 = 5/9, xi_2 = 0, w_2 = 8/9, w_3 = sqrt(3/5), xi_3 = 5/9$

Gaussian quadrature rule with $n_"int"$ points exactly integrates polynomials of order $<= 2 n_"int" - 1$




